conformist-git(7)

# NAME

conformist-git - git conventions conformist enforces

# DESCRIPTION

conformist ships two git *merge drivers* for files that a generator owns, and a
whole-tree linter that checks a repo binds the right paths to them. This page is
the normative home for those conventions.

See *conformist*(7) for conformist's concepts, *conformist-nix*(7) for the flake
rules, and *gitattributes*(5) for the underlying git mechanism.

# MERGE DRIVERS

A repo whose generated files stall rebases MUST bind those paths to the
conformist merge drivers in _.gitattributes_.

A generated file has no meaningful three-way merge. Its content is a pure
function of its inputs, so when two branches both regenerate it the right answer
is not "one of the two sides" but "whatever the generator emits for the merged
input". Git's built-in merge cannot know that, so it reports a textual conflict
on a file no human should be editing by hand.

Two shapes account for essentially all of it:

- _flake.lock_, where both sides carry a differently-stale pin of the same
  inputs. Neither side is wrong; both are just old.
- A generated source file whose only conflicting region is its
  _// Code generated by ..._ stamp, the bodies being identical.

Enforced by the *git-merge-drivers* linter, which fails when _.gitattributes_
lacks a required _<pattern> merge=<driver>_ binding, and whose repair appends
the missing lines.

## REGISTRATION IS TWO-SIDED

_.gitattributes_ alone does NOT activate a driver. Git resolves the driver name
to a command through _merge.<name>.driver_ in git config, which is registered
once per machine in eng's home-manager git configuration so the drivers are on
PATH for every clone. conformist owns only the per-repo half.

The two halves may roll out in either order. A repo that ships the
_.gitattributes_ lines before a machine has the registration is harmless: git
falls back to its ordinary three-way merge for an unknown driver name, which is
exactly the behaviour that repo had before.

## conformist-flake-lock

Bound to _flake.lock_. Seeds the lock from *ours*, then re-runs *nix flake lock*
so the result is a lock consistent with the already-merged _flake.nix_.

This is deliberately NOT a three-way reconciliation of the lock's JSON. It is
ours-biased, and the consequences are:

- An input both sides already pin keeps *ours*' revision. During a rebase ours
  is the *onto* side — the newer tip — which is the intended outcome.
- An input that only the merged _flake.nix_ newly requires is resolved fresh
  rather than left missing. This is what a plain _merge=ours_ gets wrong: it
  produces a lock that no longer matches its flake.
- Nothing else churns, because *nix flake lock* does not refresh entries that
  are already satisfied, the way *nix flake update* would.

Seeding is load-bearing rather than an optimization: regenerating from an empty
lock would re-resolve every input to latest in the middle of a merge.

## conformist-codegen-header

Bound to a repo's generated source paths. Performs an ordinary three-way merge,
then resolves *only* those conflict blocks in which both sides consist entirely
of generator-stamp lines. Any other conflict is left for a human.

Both sides of a block must be non-empty to qualify. A block where one side is
empty is a genuine addition or removal of the stamp, not a restamp, and
resolving it either way would silently add or drop the header.

The stamp pattern defaults to an optionally-commented line beginning
_Code generated_, and can be overridden with the *CONFORMIST_CODEGEN_STAMP_RE*
environment variable.

## FAILING CLOSED

Both drivers exit non-zero on any condition they cannot handle, which tells git
to record an ordinary conflict for a human to settle. A merge driver that
guesses is worse than no merge driver, because its output is believed.

*conformist-flake-lock* declines when it is outside a git worktree, when there is
no _flake.nix_ beside the lock, when that _flake.nix_ still carries conflict
markers (git gives no ordering guarantee between the two files' drivers, so a
lock regenerated against an unresolved flake is a real hazard), when *nix* is not
on PATH, and when *nix flake lock* itself fails.

*conformist-codegen-header* declines when *git merge-file* errors, and whenever a
conflict block is not stamp-only.

## SCOPE OF THE DEFAULTS

Only _flake.lock_ is bound by default, because every eng repo has one and the
driver's behaviour there is uniform.

Generated *source* globs are opt-in per repo. conformist cannot know which paths
a given repo generates, and a glob that also matched a hand-written sibling would
route real source through a driver that resolves stamp conflicts on its own.
Name the generated paths explicitly:

```
linters.git-merge-drivers.entries = [
  { pattern = "flake.lock"; driver = "conformist-flake-lock"; when-file = "flake.nix"; }
  { pattern = "stats/stats_type.go"; driver = "conformist-codegen-header"; }
];
```

*when-file* suppresses an entry unless the named file exists at the tree root,
which is what keeps the default _flake.lock_ binding silent in a non-flake repo.

